# ── Chapter 26 fidelity pins: "Clawback" ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/26-clawback.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # all substantive pins and contradiction checks
#
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] THE CIRA DEFECT IS RESOLVED — the student clawback rule is
# deleted from Article 4, exactly the repair the old :defect directive here
# named. lose(Points, Cira) now pins FALSE as a plain verdict, and the chapter's
# reader account states the current personal boundary. The
# reasoning that forced deletion rather than narrowing stays on record: students
# never mint (the grades ruling), so "claw back only recognition derived from
# the fraudulent teaching" had an empty target — narrowing was always repeal.
# The reward(Cira) and person(Fin) pins remain the chapter's sharpest facts: a
# student who never earned anything, and a student who is not on the personhood
# roster at all.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 39

# "Ask the record whether Bela is recognised and the answer is no ... Beside
# that refusal the record holds one more conclusion: a loss." Both halves of the
# corrected register, on one person: the mint refuses, and the loss is recorded.
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? reward(Bela).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Bela).
# => TRUE

# "Bela is still a person. Bela still moves freely."
? person(Bela).
# => TRUE

? travel(Bela).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "...still holds the vote. And Bela is still owed everything on the floor."
? decide(Bela, Ballot).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Eats, Bela).
# => TRUE

# THE PINS THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT THE OLD SENTENCE. The chapter used to say a
# voided person "still eats. Still has somewhere to live." The record says
# neither, about Bela or about anybody — being owed is not having, and the
# chapter that owns that gap is chapter 4. Jala is the control: never accused,
# never voided, and the same FALSE — which is what proves the verdict is the
# delivery gap and not the voiding. If eats(Bela) ever turns TRUE while
# eats(Jala) stays FALSE, something made the floor conditional on the finding,
# and both chapters are wrong.
? eats(Bela).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

? eats(Jala).
# => FALSE

# "Bela taught Cira. Bela was voided. The rule reached Cira too" — history now;
# the teaching entry is still real and still on the record.
? teaches(Bela, Cira).
# => TRUE

# [v0.9] The repair, held as a plain verdict. The rule that recorded a loss
# against Cira for Bela's fraud is deleted; no loss reaches her. This pin was
# TRUE under a :defect directive for as long as the rule stood — if it ever
# reads TRUE again, somebody rebuilt the student clawback.
? lose(Points, Cira).
# => FALSE

# "Cira earned nothing. Being taught is not a way to earn recognition here."
? reward(Cira).
# => FALSE

# Nothing reaches Cira now — not a loss (pinned above), and not her
# credibility either, which was never touched even while the student rule
# stood. If this ever flips TRUE, something taught the instrument to reach a
# student's word, and the closing section is wrong about what it can touch.
? false(Cira).
# => FALSE

# "the difference between Fin and Cira — which was never anything either of
#  them did, only a fact about their teachers — is now no difference at all."
# Fin's side of the exhibit: never reached while the rule stood, identical now.
? teaches(Esa, Fin).
# => TRUE

# and Fin, the student held up as the lucky one, is not even a person here
? person(Fin).
# => FALSE

? false(Esa).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Fin).
# => FALSE

# the same clawback reaches the other voided auditor
? false(Lupo).
# => TRUE

? lose(Points, Lupo).
# => TRUE

# and Mira, accused by a liar, keeps everything
? lose(Points, Mira).
# => FALSE

# ── The epoch carry, in the chapter whose subject it serves ──────────────────
# Vex is the one person in the cast who is voided, stripped of the pen, still
# possessed of standing, and clawed back — and this file, the clawback
# chapter's own, never touched him. The carry is why: rotten is the mark
# written forward from a prior period, it re-derives the void THIS period, and
# that is the only reason the clawback keeps firing on a person whose voiding
# nobody in this epoch performed. Five verdicts, and between them they hold
# apart three things this book keeps having to hold apart: voided is not
# de-personed, losing the pen is not losing standing, and a prior period's
# judgment costs you here and now.
? rotten(Vex).
# => TRUE

? false(Vex).
# => TRUE

# the cost — pinned in no other file
? lose(Points, Vex).
# => TRUE

# the pen goes; the standing that makes you accountable does not
? permits(Review, Vex).
# => FALSE

? authority(Vex).
# => TRUE

# ── [v0.9 2026-08-02] FORGIVENESS, MEASURED. Ground facts persist ────────────
# The expungement ruling: forgive names its body, and alone it does nothing —
# the one-write spring the clear guard closed is closed here from birth. With
# the body's judgment beside it, the mark comes clean: the void stops
# re-deriving, the loss stops, the doors reopen — and the pen stays lost,
# because the credential rules read the mark itself, which remains on the
# record. Forgiven, not erased. The retroactive consequence is pinned rather
# than discovered: Vex's recorded examination of Tyr starts paying.
forgive(Appeals, Vex).

? false(Vex).
# => TRUE

judge(Appeals, Vex).

? clean(Vex).
# => TRUE

? false(Vex).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Vex).
# => FALSE

? permits(Review, Vex).
# => FALSE

? reward(Vex).
# => TRUE

? rotten(Vex).
# => TRUE

# [v0.9 2026-08-04] L1: exercise the exact-name route the prose says is now
# closed. This block is last because the admission remains resident. That makes
# the refusal prove more than omission without widening the context of unrelated
# pins above. The mixed assertion also proves atomic rollback: the otherwise
# legal person fact must not survive the refused loss. The final probe holds the
# corpus's three-place shape beside the live two-place Bela derivation above.
# `derived_only` closes the relation, not an arity.
:accept
admits("lose").

:refuse reasoning /`lose` is declared derived-only/
person(Loss_Probe) & lose(Points, Loss_Probe).

? person(Loss_Probe).
# => FALSE

:refuse reasoning /`lose` is declared derived-only/
lose(Points, Ghost, Case).
